<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volunteers of the Faith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ghuzat al-Jihad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volunteers of the Faith (Ghuzat al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a>) were a corps of North African, mainly Zenata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, warriors who served the Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> from the late thirteenth century. Many were exiled members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> and their followers, led by their own commander, the shaykh al-ghuzat. They formed the core of Granada's army for most of the fourteenth century, until their power was curbed under Muhammad V.</p></div>
